    "result_text": "All 22 tests pass with a green build. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `namesakeCount` feature: on owner creation, the response now returns `namesakeCount` = the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same `firstName` and `lastName`, compared case-insensitively.\n\nChanges made, following the existing pattern already used for `customerCode`/`householdId`:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added the read-only `namesakeCount` (integer, `minimum: 0`) property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field.\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the `namesakeCount` field (mapped to column `namesake_count`) with getter/setter.\n\n3. **DB schemas** \u2014 Added the `namesake_count` column to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb \u2014 used by the tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres for consistency).\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 Added an `assignNamesakeCount(owner)` step (alongside `assignCustomerCode`/`assignHouseholdId`, before save) that counts existing owners matching both names case-insensitively via `equalsIgnoreCase`, and stores it. Because the count is taken before persisting, it reflects only pre-existing namesakes.\n\nMapStruct auto-maps the field onto `OwnerDto`, so it's echoed on both the create response and `GET /api/owners/{id}`. The new `Cp13Tests` passes and the full suite is green (22/22).",
